  "errata": [
    {
      "location": "TR p.12, proof of Lemma B.5 (before Eq. 50)",
      "printed": "no variable in two distinct ancestral components could be in the same c-component in G[V(A*)]",
      "adjudicated": "Under Definition A.1, copies of Y in different worlds can fall in distinct ancestral components even when they share the exogenous root of Y. In X -> Y, the cross-world distribution for Y_x0 and Y_x1 therefore need not factorize as in Eq. 48."
    },
    {
      "location": "TR p.13, soundness proof of Theorem 2.5",
      "printed": "Consider the ancestral factorization Theorem B.5 of W* = {Y_r, X_t} union Z* given Z* ...",
      "adjudicated": "Invalid: the argument never uses G_A or the d-separation hypothesis, and with its implicit premise it would also prove Y_x0 _||_ Y_x1 in X -> Y. The soundness statement is re-derived in docs/reference/COUNTERFACTUAL_CALCULUS.md from the global Markov property of the merged parallel-worlds DAG."
    },
    {
      "location": "TR p.6 Theorem 2.5 'Moreover' clause and text; p.1 abstract; p.2 contribution 1; p.7 Table 1; p.9 conclusions; proof pp.13-14",
      "printed": "if an independence relationship cannot be inferred with the criterion, there exist at least two models inducing the graphical model where the independence does not hold",
      "adjudicated": "False on fixed binary domains: case binary_domain_gap holds in every model with binary X but is not d-separated in G_A. The p.13 construction is binary, so it cannot witness the dependence; the AMWN criterion is implemented as sound only."
    },
    {
      "location": "TR p.11, proof of Lemma B.2",
      "printed": "does not depend on any X in Z \\ X",
      "adjudicated": "Typo for X \\ Z; the exclusion lemma itself is re-derived and holds unit by unit."
    },
    {
      "location": "TR p.6, walkthrough of Figure 6 after Algorithm 1",
      "printed": "The corresponding AWMN is shown in Figure 6(b). The node Uz has been added and connected to Z and Zw (line 1) ... There is also the node Uzx, that is connected to Z, Zw and X due to the bidirected arrow Z <-> X in G (line 1). By the d-separation criterion, the path X <-> Zw -> Yxw is active given {Z, W}",
      "adjudicated": "Harmless slips. Algorithm 1 adds U_z at line 3 and U_zx at line 6, not at line 1; AWMN is a typo for AMWN; and in G_A the printed X <-> Z_w stands for X <- U_zx -> Z_w. The stated dependence holds: case r115_fig6_dependence executes it and network case r115_figure6b reproduces the figure."
    }
  ],
